 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nColumn Qualification Issue in SQL Optimization\n\nI've encountered an issue with column qualification in the SQL optimization process. When attempting to qualify columns in a query, the optimizer seems to misidentify or fail to qualify certain columns, leading to unexpected behavior.\n\nHere's a simple reproduction script:\n\n```python\nfrom sqlglot import parse_one, optimizer\n\nquery = '''\n    SELECT Teams.Name, count(*)\n    FROM raw.TeamMemberships as TeamMemberships\n    JOIN raw.Teams\n    ON Teams.Id = TeamMemberships.TeamId\n    GROUP BY 1\n'''\n\nschema = {\n    'raw': {\n        'TeamMemberships': {'Id': 'INTEGER', 'UserId': 'INTEGER', 'TeamId': 'INTEGER'},\n        'Teams': {'Id': 'INTEGER', 'Name': 'STRING'}\n    }\n}\n\noptimized_query = optimizer.qualify.qualify(parse_one(query, read='bigquery'), schema=schema, dialect='bigquery').sql(dialect='bigquery')\nprint(optimized_query)\n```\n\nExpected output:\n```\nSELECT `teams`.`name` AS `name`, count(*) AS `_col_1` FROM `raw`.`TeamMemberships` AS `teammemberships` JOIN `raw`.`Teams` AS `teams` ON `teams`.`id` = `teammemberships`.`teamid` GROUP BY `teams`.`name`\n```\n\nActual output:\n```\n<Error or unexpected SQL output>\n```\n\nThe issue seems to arise when the optimizer attempts to qualify columns without a clear table reference, especially in cases involving joins or nested queries. This behavior is inconsistent with previous versions where such queries were handled correctly.\n\nI'm using Python 3.10.16 and the latest version of sqlglot. Any insights or suggestions on how to address this would be greatly appreciated!\n</issue>\n",
